\section*{Solution to Chapter 3, Question 9}

\subsection*{(a) Scatterplot Matrix}
We plot all quantitative variables in the \texttt{Auto} data.
\[
\text{Observation: } \text{mpg decreases with weight, horsepower, and displacement; mpg increases with year.}
\]

\subsection*{(b) Correlation Matrix}
\[
\text{Cor}(mpg, weight) \approx -0.83, \quad \text{Cor}(mpg, year) \approx 0.58.
\]

\subsection*{(c) Multiple Linear Regression}
Model:
\[
mpg_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \text{cylinders}_i + \beta_2 \text{displacement}_i +
\beta_3 \text{horsepower}_i + \beta_4 \text{weight}_i +
\beta_5 \text{acceleration}_i + \beta_6 \text{year}_i +
\beta_7 \text{origin}_i + \varepsilon_i.
\]
Significant predictors: \(\text{weight}, \text{year}, \text{origin}\).
\[
\hat{\beta}_{year} > 0 \implies \text{newer cars are more fuel-efficient.}
\]

\subsection*{(d) Diagnostic Plots}
Residual plots show approximate linearity and constant variance.  
Some high-leverage points appear (Cook’s distance).

\subsection*{(e) Interaction Effects}
Including an interaction between year and weight:
\[
mpg_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \text{weight}_i + \beta_2 \text{year}_i +
\beta_3 (\text{weight}_i \times \text{year}_i) + \varepsilon_i.
\]
The interaction term is significant, implying that the negative effect of weight
on fuel efficiency diminishes for newer models.

\subsection*{(f) Transformations}
Transforming predictors, e.g. \(\log(\text{weight})\) and \(\sqrt{\text{horsepower}}\),
improves model fit and reduces heteroskedasticity:
\[
mpg_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \log(\text{weight}_i) + \beta_2 \sqrt{\text{horsepower}_i} + \beta_3 \text{year}_i + \varepsilon_i.
\]
\textbf{Conclusion:} lighter, newer cars achieve higher mileage; transformed
predictors yield a slightly improved model.

